{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":657.5499877929688,"text":"They don't just automate, they redesign. And studies show that the cohort of high performers are 75% more likely, or in practice, they actually do redesign their workflow versus 25% of the cohort that doesn't. So where does this leave us with our two hypothetical companies and designing the right problem? Company B adds AI to existing workflows, they're completing individual projects and they're moving to the next initiative, whereas company A is rethinking how work is done. They're redesigning processes around AI and they're obsessing over the right problem. That's the difference in how organizations put AI to work. Great, so they've picked the right problem. What happens next? We all know in order to actually enable that capacity, you need to build share capabilities across the org. What's a trend that's seemingly emerging for some time now is that the lines between technology and business are converging. We're seeing this everywhere. We're seeing engineers that are moving closer to business outcomes, we're seeing business professionals that are building tools and automations. As you can see and imagine, you can have forward deployed engineers, you can have forward deployed product managers, but you could also have the inverse, which is forward deployed portfolio managers. And it's really this combination of getting the right people in the room by breaking down organization silos that the leaders are really leaning on. Studies have shown that more and more of the large enterprises are viewing their technology arm as a business growth function. And that's creating an edge for these companies. So the other sort of vertical inside building shared capabilities that is probably a personal favorite of mine, it's standardizing the core and personalizing the edges. And this is something that isn't new to the technology world. We build infrastructure, we build shared modules and system design, but what's interesting here today is that's taking a very different shape with the capabilities out there and with the org and the breaking down of silos. And this could look very different depending on where you sit. You could be building shared cores for a team that looks very different with analysts and clients. You could be doing that with a bunch of analysts or you could be doing that for software engineers or data scientists. With the data scientist example in particular, that could look very interesting where instead of having one-off workflows that is really common in the industry, you could build a shared core of the data sets, their semantic layers, the trade-offs of what worked, what didn't, what some data sets do good off, where are the gaps and so on and so forth, such that each new personnel becomes almost a plug-and-play module in the system where they can then stand on the shoulder of their predecessors. So what should the next person not have to rebuild? Now what's also interesting is the enterprise wasn't really built for agents. They work across workflows and applications, they take context, they deliberate, they take actions, whereas really the enterprises are focused previously on single human or a collection of them interacting with a single application. So the leaders are cognizant that something needs to change and that they need to modernize their infrastructure and they're very self-aware of where they are in their journey. And so studies show that 71% of the orgs are modernizing their core infrastructure to support AI and 6 to 10% or 23% of them are investing 6 to 10% of their annual revenue in core system modernization to take advantage of this. And the common complaint here or a common challenge here is data quality and the ability to reuse data. So great, we have the right people in the room, we have the right knowledge there. What do we do next? And then comes the concept of, hey, how do we make this knowledge reusable and accessible? And that's where we have, depending on the adoption and where they are, you can have different types of team agents where you can have expert agents being subject matter experts, you can have workflow agents that work across streams like Slack bots that are creating and opening tickets and closing them and what have you, and then you can have really chief of staff kind of agents that keep a tab on what the state of the organization is and what needs to be done. So going back to our workflows, A, again, is looking at this very differently in terms of shared capabilities. Now, the last pillar here that I want to draw your attention to is, well, when does this system really comp on? What happens when it's deployed? Well, leading companies, what they're doing is they're actually thinking about it slightly differently. They're thinking about failures that can give us an edge that we can learn from. If there's a success, how do we make it part of the system? And the way they think about this is, you know, the individual learned but did the system. Is there a way, perhaps, when they've encountered something where they need to make a correction, that that knowledge is fed back into the org, either through a new tool or context or what have you? And what's key here is preserving the evidence along with those learnings. So really, the last vertical here is AI-free use capacity. Where does it go? Being cognizant that you need to redesign your system and really focus it on key metrics that you're evaluating, you're monitoring, and you're creating a flywheel off. So where does this leave us for companies A and B? Company A has their entire work redesigned. They're building capabilities that compound, and they have shared capabilities built. So I want to leave you off with one final question. Everyone has AI. Who has the advantage? Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.  Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you for watching.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":2.559999942779541,"text":" They don't just automate, they redesign.","tokens":[50364,814,500,380,445,31605,11,436,39853,13,50492],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3148731589317322,"compression_ratio":1.5920000076293945,"no_speech_prob":0.032567836344242096},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":2.559999942779541,"end":9.720000267028809,"text":" And studies show that the cohort of high performers are 75% more likely, or in practice, they actually","tokens":[50492,400,5313,855,300,264,28902,295,1090,30768,366,9562,4,544,3700,11,420,294,3124,11,436,767,50850],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3148731589317322,"compression_ratio":1.5920000076293945,"no_speech_prob":0.032567836344242096},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":9.720000267028809,"end":16.040000915527344,"text":" do redesign their workflow versus 25% of the cohort that doesn't.","tokens":[50850,360,39853,641,20993,5717,3552,4,295,264,28902,300,1177,380,13,51166],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3148731589317322,"compression_ratio":1.5920000076293945,"no_speech_prob":0.032567836344242096},{"id":3,"seek":0,"start":16.040000915527344,"end":22.15999984741211,"text":" So where does this leave us with our two hypothetical companies and designing the right problem?","tokens":[51166,407,689,775,341,1856,505,365,527,732,33053,3431,293,14685,264,558,1154,30,51472],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3148731589317322,"compression_ratio":1.5920000076293945,"no_speech_prob":0.032567836344242096},{"id":4,"seek":0,"start":22.15999984741211,"end":26.959999084472656,"text":" Company B adds AI to existing workflows, they're completing individual projects and they're","tokens":[51472,13918,363,10860,7318,281,6741,43461,11,436,434,19472,2609,4455,293,436,434,51712],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.3148731589317322,"compression_ratio":1.5920000076293945,"no_speech_prob":0.032567836344242096},{"id":5,"seek":2696,"start":26.959999084472656,"end":32.279998779296875,"text":" moving to the